{
  "teamId": 121,
  "year": 2024,
  "narrative": "The 2024 New York Mets were a team that refused to die, clawing their way from an ugly 0-3 start and a catastrophic May collapse to ultimately secure a postseason berth and make a genuine October run. The early signs were encouraging — Pete Alonso, Mark Vientos, and Francisco Lindor delivered critical early-season blasts — but the bullpen, and Edwin Díaz in particular, proved maddeningly unreliable throughout the year, surrendering lead after lead in ways that made the Mets' winning record feel harder-earned than it should have.\n\nMay was the darkest month, a 9-19 slog defined by Díaz surrendering home runs to Randy Arozarena and Josh Bell, Reed Garrett giving up a Patrick Bailey grand slam, and the offense sputtering at the worst moments. Yet the team never fully capsized, and June arrived like a breath of fresh air. J.D. Martinez, the veteran designated hitter, emerged as an unlikely hero, slugging two pivotal home runs that helped carry the Mets to a 16-8 June and restore a sense of order. Harrison Bader's clutch doubles and Francisco Lindor's consistency in big spots gave the lineup a resilience that papered over the bullpen's volatility.\n\nJuly and August were a grind — winning months on the whole, but punctuated by Díaz meltdowns and Huascar Brazobán coughing up a three-run lead in Anaheim. The Mets kept winning ugly, riding their plus-run-differential to 89 wins and a Wild Card slot. Then came September's emotional crescendo: Francisco Lindor, in the second-to-last series of the regular season, crushed his 33rd home run to help punch the Mets' ticket, a fitting exclamation point from the captain.\n\nThe October story was equal parts heartbreak and magic. After surviving a rocky Wild Card series against the Brewers — Phil Maton was touched up twice before Pete Alonso's three-run blast in Game 2 turned the tide — the Mets pushed past the Philadelphia Phillies in a dramatic NLDS that came down to late-inning tension, with Mark Vientos and Lindor delivering clutch hits while Díaz and the pen were tested to their limits. The run ended in the NLCS, one round short of the World Series, but for a team that was 9-19 in May, reaching that stage felt like its own kind of triumph.",
  "chapters": [
